     * Sets the standard discount on new opportunity line items whose Discount
     * is null. Lines with a Discount already set (including 0) are left unchanged.
     * Bulk-safe: one SOQL query per invocation regardless of line count.
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> lines) {
        if (lines == null || lines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : lines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Map<Id, String> familyByProductId = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Product2 product : [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]) {
            familyByProductId.put(product.Id, product.Family);
        }

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : lines) {
            if (line.Discount != null || line.Product2Id == null) {
                continue;
            }
            String family = familyByProductId.get(line.Product2Id);
            line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family);
        }
    }
}
```

The nested loop would work for these sizes, but I'd use a map instead. Looping over every contact for every lead is up to 9 million comparisons, and each one normalizes two strings. That can use a large share of the synchronous CPU budget inside the import transaction. Building a map once from the contacts makes the work linear: one pass over the contacts, one over the leads. The map also handles "first in `contacts` order" cleanly, because the first contact to claim a key is kept and later duplicates are ignored. Because the method only reads its inputs, it issues no SOQL or DML and runs with sharing enforced.

